    “Every shell on this wall is trying to predict something,” Sofía said.
She was rotating the laptop in a slow quarter-circle, like she was read‐
ing a map. “The grant, the playlist, the permits. Somebody has a model
of this block, and I think they’ve been checking it since you were six.
And the thing about a model is, it has to be right. That’s the job. That’s
what scares me.” She closed the laptop halfway, which from Sofía is a
gesture of love on the order of a blood transfusion. “People who need
to be right don’t stop.”
    I kept my mouth shut. Anything I said, I’d have to answer for.
    “Don’t reply to the email,” she said finally. “Don’t delete it. And
don’t walk home past the bodega.”
    “Why?”
    “Because I just spent an hour telling you I don’t believe in your loop,
and I’d like to still be wrong by tomorrow.” She smiled with half her
mouth. “Also, eat a vegetable.”



I walked home past the bodega.
    Obviously. It’s on the corner of my block. It’s also the only route
from the library that doesn’t involve crossing under the El in the dark.
That’s what I told myself. The real reason was that Sofía had told me
not to, and I’m a person who has to put a hand on a stove just to see if
it’s hot.
